<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scipio Africanus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">236–183 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Publius Cornelius Scipio Africanus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Publius Cornelius Scipio Africanus was the Roman general who defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. He captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a> in 209 BCE, won at Ilipa in 206 BCE, driving the Carthaginians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, and in 204 BCE invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> as ally. His victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> at Zama in 202 BCE ended the war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus.jpg" alt="Scipio Africanus" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Luiz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Sulla_(loan_from_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek)_-_Glyptothek_-_Munich_-_Germany_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Celtiberians served widely as mercenaries: in 211 BCE some 20,000 hired by Rome deserted Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio, contributing to the destruction of both <b>Scipio</b> brothers' armies, and about 4,000 fought for Carthage at the Battle of the Great Plains in 203 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Masinissa, son of the Massylian king Gaia, was raised partly at Carthage and fought for the Carthaginians in Spain, but in 206 BCE he went over to Rome and its general <b>Publius Cornelius Scipio</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>But most of Rome’s Latin and Italian allies stayed loyal even after Cannae, Rome adopted the delaying strategy associated with Fabius Maximus, and the Scipio family, above all <b>Publius Cornelius Scipio</b>, destroyed Carthaginian power in Spain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Second Punic War Hannibal destroyed Roman armies at the Trebia, Lake Trasimene and Cannae in 218–216 BCE, but Rome refused terms, and <b>Publius Cornelius Scipio Africanus</b> defeated him at the Battle of Zama in 202 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Scipio Africanus</b> prepared his invasion of Africa in 205 BCE, Livy records, Caere sent grain, Populonia iron, Tarquinia linen for sails and Arretium shields, helmets and spears.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome sent Gnaeus and Publius Cornelius Scipio, who both died in the fighting, and then <b>Publius Cornelius Scipio Africanus</b>, who arrived in 210 BCE and won at Baecula and Ilipa.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Liternum, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-202" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Scipio Africanus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-202">What was here in 202 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But if these things also appear oppressive, prepare for war, since you could not brook the conditions of peace.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to Hannibal before Zama (Livy, History of Rome, book 30) &#183; tr. Cyrus R. Edmonds, 1850 &#183; Livy's account of their meeting, 202 BCE &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There is not, believe me, there is not so much danger to be apprehended by persons at our time of life from armed foes, as from the pleasures which surround us on all sides.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to Masinissa (Livy, History of Rome, book 30) &#183; tr. Cyrus R. Edmonds, 1850 &#183; Livy's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The man who by temperance has curbed and subdued his appetite for them, has acquired for himself much greater honour and a much more important victory than we now enjoy in the conquest of Syphax.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to Masinissa (Livy, History of Rome, book 30) &#183; tr. Cyrus R. Edmonds, 1850 &#183; Livy's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12582/pg12582-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
